Task 1: Check Current I/O Scheduler
Subtask 1.1: Install Required Tools
sudo dnf install sysstat  # For RHEL/CentOS/Fedora
# OR
sudo apt install sysstat  # For Debian/Ubuntu
Subtask 1.2: Check Active I/O Schedulers
# List available schedulers for your block devices
ls /sys/block/sd*/queue/scheduler

# Check current scheduler for all disks
for disk in /sys/block/sd*; do
  echo "Disk: ${disk##*/}, Scheduler: $(cat $disk/queue/scheduler)"
done
Expected Output:
You'll see output similar to:

Disk: sda, Scheduler: [mq-deadline] kyber bfq none
(The one in brackets is the active scheduler)

Subtask 1.3: Monitor I/O Statistics
# Monitor disk I/O in real-time (refresh every 2 seconds)
iostat -x 2
Key Metrics to Observe:

%util - Percentage of CPU time spent on I/O
await - Average time for I/O requests to be served
svctm - Average service time for I/O requests
Task 2: Change I/O Scheduler
Subtask 2.1: Change Scheduler Temporarily
# Change scheduler for sda to mq-deadline (example)
echo 'mq-deadline' | sudo tee /sys/block/sda/queue/scheduler

# Verify change
cat /sys/block/sda/queue/scheduler
Troubleshooting Tip:
If you get "Permission denied", ensure you're using sudo or have root privileges.

Subtask 2.2: Make Changes Persistent
Create or edit the following file:

sudo nano /etc/udev/rules.d/60-io-schedulers.rules
Add this line (adjust sda and scheduler as needed):

ACTION=="add|change", KERNEL=="sda", ATTR{queue/scheduler}="mq-deadline"
Apply changes:

sudo udevadm control --reload-rules
sudo udevadm trigger
Task 3: Test Different I/O Schedulers
Subtask 3.1: Common Scheduler Types
mq-deadline: Good for most workloads, ensures fairness
bfq: Better for desktop systems, prioritizes interactivity
kyber: Optimized for fast devices (SSDs/NVMe)
none: No scheduling (for specialized cases)
Subtask 3.2: Performance Testing
Create a test file and measure performance with different schedulers:

# Create 1GB test file
dd if=/dev/zero of=testfile bs=1M count=1024 conv=fdatasync

# Test read performance (run for each scheduler)
echo 3 | sudo tee /proc/sys/vm/drop_caches  # Clear cache
time dd if=testfile of=/dev/null bs=1M
Expected Outcome:
Compare the real time reported by time for different schedulers. Results will vary based on your hardware.

Subtask 3.3: Workload-Specific Testing
For database-like workload:

fio --name=random-write --ioengine=posixaio --rw=randwrite --bs=4k \
    --size=1g --numjobs=1 --iodepth=1 --runtime=60 --time_based --end_fsync=1
For sequential workload:

fio --name=seq-read --ioengine=posixaio --rw=read --bs=1M \
    --size=1g --numjobs=1 --iodepth=32 --runtime=60 --time_based
Analysis:
Compare the IOPS (I/O Operations Per Second) and bandwidth reported by fio for different schedulers.

